-- Azerbaijan deep-dive content (English). A mature, universal B2B
-- e-invoicing regime (elektron qaimə-faktura / e-qaimə) in force
-- since 1 April 2017 and universal since 1 January 2018, with the
-- best primary-source footing of any country added in this recent
-- run: the State Tax Service publishes its own e-invoicing page, user
-- guide, sanctions booklet and annual amendments FAQ, all of which
-- were fetched directly.
--
-- Editorial position on the compliance model: three separate industry
-- sources give three incompatible labels for this system ("post-audit
-- reporting", "post-audit clearance", "clearance model"). Rather than
-- pick one, this page describes the mechanism precisely -- the State
-- Tax Service issues the invoice's series and number and records it
-- in AVIS at issuance, but does not substantively approve it, and the
-- buyer confirms within five days with silent auto-confirmation. That
-- is a registration-at-issuance CTC model. The disagreement is
-- surfaced in its own card rather than hidden.
--
-- Azerbaijan has a genuine, quantified, escalating penalty schedule
-- published by the tax authority itself, so this build uses a real
-- penalty table -- unlike Uzbekistan alongside it, which has only one
-- figure and gets narrative cards.
--
-- Flagged gaps stated on the page: no e-invoice volume statistics
-- appear to be published by Azerbaijan at all (no adoption figure is
-- invented here); no statutory e-invoice retention period could be
-- confirmed; B2G scope is inferred rather than primary-sourced; and
-- the advance-payment invoice's article number is cited differently
-- by two sources.

  'Centralised CTC, registration-at-issuance: the State Tax Service portal issues the invoice number and records the invoice in AVİS as it is issued, but does not substantively approve it — the buyer confirms within five days, with silent auto-confirmation',
  'This deep-dive page reflects Azerbaijani rules as published by the State Tax Service (taxes.gov.az) — including its e-qaimə page, 2024 user guide, 2025 financial-sanctions booklet and 2026 amendments FAQ — together with Cabinet of Ministers decision texts on nk.gov.az, as of 10 August 2026. The consolidated Tax Code is served as a single very large document that could not be retrieved in full, so nothing here quotes statutory text verbatim; each claim rests instead on an official State Tax Service publication or a Cabinet decision. Provided for general awareness, not legal or tax advice. Confirm current requirements with the State Tax Service or an Azerbaijani tax adviser before relying on this page.',
  'Azerbaijan''s e-qaimə mandate began on 1 April 2017 under Cabinet Decision No. 89, implementing Tax Code Article 71-1, and extended to all remaining taxpayers on 1 January 2018. In 2020 the separate electronic VAT invoice was abolished and merged into the e-qaimə. Cabinet Decision No. 26 of 2021 is the current rulebook. New timing rules took effect on 1 January 2026.',
  'A compliant e-qaimə is an XML document drawn up in Azerbaijani, signed with an enhanced electronic signature, transmitted to the registered recipient through the State Tax Service portal and recorded in AVİS, the Automated Tax Information System. The State Tax Service publishes the file-format specification, sample templates and a goods, works and services classification file. Signing uses ASAN İMZA, e-İmza or SİMA Token, producing .adoc or .edoc containers.',
  'The mandate covers essentially the whole B2B commercial-supply population, with no revenue threshold on the obligation itself since the 2018 phase. B2C is out of scope as a rule — retail runs on new-generation cash registers instead — though a consumer may request an e-invoice in a retail or catering transaction. Persons not registered as taxpayers cannot issue electronic invoices at all, and non-residents do not issue Azerbaijani e-invoices.',
  'If you supply goods, works or services in Azerbaijan as a registered taxpayer, you are already in scope and have been since 2018. The live work is the 1 January 2026 timing change, which is genuinely disruptive for anyone billing continuous services on a quarterly or annual cycle, and which most international compliance summaries have not yet picked up.',
  'Azerbaijan publishes a real, quantified and escalating penalty schedule for e-invoicing failures, and it bites both sides of the transaction: the seller for not issuing, and the buyer for holding goods without the required invoices.',
  'Azerbaijan has required electronic invoices since 1 April 2017, universal for all taxpayers from 1 January 2018, under Tax Code Article 71-1 and Cabinet Decision No. 89. Invoices are XML, drawn up in Azerbaijani, signed with an enhanced electronic signature and registered in the State Tax Service''s AVİS system, which assigns the series and number; the buyer then has five days to confirm, with silence counting as confirmation. From 1 January 2026 international transport and continuous services lost the general five-day invoicing window.',
  '🇦🇿'
FROM countries WHERE code = 'AZ';









